WebSockets — concept + Spring Boot (interview)

Used for real-time client ↔ server: chat, live notifications, stock ticks, dashboards, multiplayer games.

────────────────────────────────────────
1. What is WebSocket?

Protocol for **full-duplex** messaging over one **persistent TCP** connection.

HTTP (typical API style):
Client → Request
Server → Response
(Interaction is request/response; connection may be reused via keep-alive, but server doesn’t freely push messages)

WebSocket:
Client ↔ Server (continuous two-way)
Connection stays open after upgrade
Server can **push** anytime without client polling

────────────────────────────────────────
2. How connection starts

1️⃣ Client sends HTTP upgrade request:

GET /chat
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade

2️⃣ Server accepts:

HTTP 101 Switching Protocols

3️⃣ Connection becomes WebSocket (ws:// or wss://)

Client ↔ Server — both send anytime

────────────────────────────────────────
3. Spring Boot support (important correction)

`spring-boot-starter-websocket` supports:

A) Raw WebSocket (`WebSocketHandler` / `WebSocketConfigurer`)
B) STOMP messaging over WebSocket (very common for chat apps)

STOMP is **not** the WebSocket protocol itself —
it’s a simple messaging protocol that often runs **on top of** WebSocket
(destinations like `/app`, `/topic`).

Dependency:

<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-websocket</artifactId>
</dependency>

────────────────────────────────────────
4. STOMP configuration (common interview example)

@Configuration
@EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class WebSocketConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {

    @Override
    public void registerStompEndpoints(StompEndpointRegistry registry) {
        registry.addEndpoint("/chat")
                .setAllowedOriginPatterns("*")  // prefer patterns in modern Spring
                .withSockJS(); // optional fallback for older browsers
    }

    @Override
    public void configureMessageBroker(MessageBrokerRegistry registry) {
        registry.enableSimpleBroker("/topic");          // subscribe / broadcast
        registry.setApplicationDestinationPrefixes("/app"); // client → server
    }
}

Meaning:
/chat   → WebSocket (SockJS) endpoint to connect
/app    → client sends here (e.g. /app/send)
/topic  → clients subscribe for broadcasts (e.g. /topic/messages)

`enableSimpleBroker` = in-memory broker (fine for demos).
Production often uses an external broker (RabbitMQ/Redis relay) for scale.

────────────────────────────────────────
5. Controller example

@Controller
public class ChatController {

    @MessageMapping("/send")
    @SendTo("/topic/messages")
    public String sendMessage(String message) {
        return message;
    }
}

Flow:
Client send → /app/send
  → @MessageMapping("/send")
  → @SendTo("/topic/messages")
  → all subscribers receive

────────────────────────────────────────
6. JavaScript client (STOMP + SockJS)

var socket = new SockJS('/chat');
var stompClient = Stomp.over(socket);

stompClient.connect({}, function () {
    stompClient.subscribe('/topic/messages', function (message) {
        console.log(message.body);
    });
    stompClient.send("/app/send", {}, "Hello Server");
});

Native WebSocket without STOMP would be:
new WebSocket("ws://localhost:8080/chat")
(only if you expose a raw WS endpoint, not the STOMP/SockJS setup above)

────────────────────────────────────────
7. Chat flow

User A sends
  ↓
Server receives
  ↓
Broadcast to /topic/...
  ↓
Users B, C, D get it instantly
(no HTTP polling)

────────────────────────────────────────
8. WebSocket vs HTTP

| Feature        | HTTP                         | WebSocket                |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Interaction    | Request / response           | Two-way messaging        |
| Server push    | Needs poll / SSE / long-poll | Natural                  |
| Connection     | Often short-lived per call*  | Persistent after upgrade |
| Latency        | Higher for frequent updates  | Low for live streams     |
| Use case       | REST APIs, CRUD              | Real-time apps           |

\*HTTP keep-alive/HTTP2 can reuse TCP; model is still request–response.

────────────────────────────────────────
9. Microservice pattern

Order Service → Kafka → Notification Service → WebSocket → Browser

Backend events fan out; WS is the last mile to the UI.

────────────────────────────────────────
10. Interview Q

Q: When should we use WebSockets?

A: When you need real-time **bidirectional** communication and server push —
chat, live notifications, market ticks, multiplayer games, live dashboards.
Unlike HTTP polling, WebSocket keeps a persistent connection so the server can push instantly.

Also know: Spring often uses **STOMP over WebSocket** for pub/sub destinations (`/app`, `/topic`).
